# Preserve the raw body and expose a JSON body's fields at the top level.
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# When the syslog body is a JSON document, RSigma parses it into fields, which
# json_content matches, but keeps no raw string. So a content / pcre /
# meta_content search on the raw body, which Sagan runs against the raw JSON
# text, has nothing to run against and is refused (E_RAW_TEXT_ON_JSON_EVENT).
#
# What it does
# ------------
# Run first, this transform keeps the original body in .sagan_raw and, when it is
# JSON, lifts the object's keys to the top level. Both searches then work:
#
#   * json_content -> the parsed top-level field (e.g. .eventName)
#   * content / pcre / meta_content on the raw body -> .sagan_raw, the original
#     string Sagan itself searched
#
# A non-JSON body is left as-is, with .sagan_raw set to the message, so the
# transform is harmless for plain events.
#
# Output
# ------
#   .sagan_raw        the original message string, always
#   .<json keys>      when the body is JSON, its keys lifted to the top level

.sagan_raw = .message

parsed, err = parse_json(.message)
if err == null && is_object(parsed) {
    # Lift the JSON keys to the top level, but let the syslog envelope and
    # .sagan_raw win on any name clash, so appname / hostname / .sagan_raw are
    # never clobbered by a same-named key inside the body.
    . = merge(object!(parsed), .)
}
